{-
   The program condition 2 of the copy-to-alias elision exists for - Analysis-IR-JS.md §6.3, and
   `doc/progress/2026-09-22-J72-jsir-alias-scalarize.md` §7's first **zero** row, closed.

   J72 weakened each safety condition in turn and two of the seven broke nothing. The one it wrote
   down as "a `Value` place root accepted as stable" is a weakening of a test that reads

       if(place.kind != JsPlaceKind::Var) return false;

   and the reason weakening *that particular arm* of it changes nothing is that the arm is
   unreachable: a whole-value read of a `Value` place is the owner value itself and not a `Load`, so
   the only roots this test is ever asked about are a `Property`, an `Element`, a `Cell` and a
   `Global`. Those are the storage it genuinely refuses and this is the fixture for them.

   `both(h.p, h)` reads a `Point` out of a field and hands the same record to a position the callee
   writes through. The copy at the borrow is what keeps them apart: `h.p.x = 100` inside the callee
   lands in `h`'s own object, and what the first argument holds was taken before it. Accepting a
   `Property` root as stable storage makes this answer **102** under Node against the 3 the native
   build answers - the read-only walk over *this* body's names proves nothing about a field, because
   the write is through a second name the callee holds.

   This fixture and `AliasWrite.yana` were once compiled by the rewrite alone, for one reason:
   `f(x, &y)` naming one storage was a program the shipping stage did not agree with itself about
   between `-no-ir-opt` and an optimized build.
-}

data Point {x: Int, y: Int}
-- Two fields, so that the record is not the transparent one-field form - `Holder {p: Point}` alone
-- *is* its `Point` here, and `h.p` would be `h`.
data Holder {p: Point, tag: Int}

-- The shared parameter and the exclusive one reach the same object at the one call below.
@noinline fn both(a: Point, &h: Holder) -> Int:
    h.p.x = 100
    return a.x + a.y

fn main() -> Int:
    let &h = Holder {p: Point {x: 1, y: 2}, tag: 0}

    -- 3: what `both` saw is the copy, made before it wrote through `h`. An alias answers 102.
    return both(h.p, h)
